# installer/MANIFEST — the ORDER in which scripts/assemble-droste-setup.sh
# concatenates the fragments to produce droste-setup.sh.
#
# One filename per line, relative to installer/. Blank lines and lines starting
# with `#` are ignored. THIS FILE IS THE ONLY SOURCE OF ORDER: the fragments
# carry no numeric prefixes, because a prefix and a manifest that disagree give
# two answers to one question. The assembler checks the list against the
# directory in BOTH directions, so a fragment that is added and not listed here
# fails loudly instead of vanishing from the artifact.
#
# ── The five ordering constraints ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything between the prologue and the tail is declarations and is therefore
# order-free. These five are not, and re-ordering the list must not break them:
#
#   1. `set -euo pipefail` comes first                            [contract]
#   2. the display mode settles BEFORE the option loop — usage() and the
#      unknown-option error are themselves output, so the --ascii pre-pass and
#      the typography atoms have to exist before the loop that consumes argv
#                                                                 [ui]
#   3. usage() is defined before the option loop runs             [ui]
#   4. the palette span is one block and must not be split        [ui]
#   5. `main` is the LAST LINE of the artifact, byte for byte — four g1lab
#      suites do `source <(sed '$d' "$SETUP")` and the whole in-shell test
#      idiom rests on it                                          [main]
#
# ── Why these fourteen ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each fragment is one or more CONTIGUOUS `# ── ` sections of the original file,
# grouped by what changes together. The one boundary that rests on a
# MEASUREMENT rather than on taste is `ui`: the display/prompt layer was
# measured at 157 inbound edges and 0 outbound, and scripts/check-installer-
# layering.sh keeps it that way. `contract` holds the two values that layer is
# told about us (UI_PROG, UI_INPUT_VAR), so the kit itself names nothing of ours.
#
# `execute`, `pull` and `ladder` are three fragments and not two because the
# pull sits physically BETWEEN the Executing section's output helpers and the
# ladder rungs that drive it, and a fragment is a contiguous range.

contract.sh
ui.sh
state.sh
preflight.sh
cfg.sh
existing.sh
interview.sh
relocate.sh
emit.sh
execute.sh
pull.sh
ladder.sh
report.sh
main.sh
